Understanding Git Workflow Fundamentals
When working with Git, code changes move through three local states before reaching a remote repository like GitHub:

Working Directory: The actual files on your system where you write code or add documents.

Staging Area (Index): A buffer area where Git organizes and prepares files before saving them into history.

Local Repository (.git folder): The internal database where Git permanently stores snapshot commits on your local machine.

Remote Repository (GitHub): The online version of your repository where team members or backup services access your commits.

Core Git Operations & Theory
1. Staging (git add .)
Theory: When you create or modify files, Git detects them as untracked or modified. Running git add copies these changes from your Working Directory into the Staging Area.

Why it matters: It lets you selectively choose which changes are ready to be packaged together into a single commit rather than forcing every unsaved edit into history at once.

2. Committing (git commit -m "message")
Theory: A commit takes a snapshot of the staged files and writes it to your local repository as a unique, immutable object tagged with a hash ID, author details, and timestamp.

Why it matters: It builds a permanent version history. If something breaks later, you can inspect or revert back to any previous commit point.

3. Pushing (git push)
Theory: git push transfers commits from your local repository branch (e.g., main) to the matching branch on your remote host (origin/main).

Why it matters: It keeps your remote GitHub repository updated so your code is backed up and accessible to collaborators.

4. Configuration (git config)
Theory: Git records user identity (name and email) on every commit object to track who made specific changes.

Global vs. Local: Setting git config --global applies your identity system-wide for all repositories, whereas setting git config without --global overrides settings only within the current project folder.
